Lucky Sevens: rebuild the lyric timings, add the section-span track

The gapless export lost its instrumentals. It turns out to be recoverable, and
this is the rebuild plus the parallel span track you asked for.

**Why it was recoverable:** the export set each line's start to the previous
line's end, which is lossy in one direction only — ends are intact, starts are
derived. So every swallowed instrumental is still visible as an inflated
duration on the line *after* it. Estimating what each line needs (median 14.27
chars/sec) and giving the rest back recovers all of them, and all four [break]s
and the [✨] screed fell out exactly where your lyric sheet says they should be.
That agreement is the check that the method is right.

Files: lyrics.json (contiguous 0 -> 550.838, empty strings where the music plays
alone), spans.json (section titles, plain {start,end,text} to match), and
spans-annotated.json (adds kind + which bottle each weld names).

As asked: [break] takes the seam and reports as an empty string, the [✨] repeat
is inserted and reads [radio screed] in the spans, and These Aye-Ayes is now
These Aye-Is.

Your four corrections are applied and live in one CORRECTIONS table at the top
of build-timings.py — add a row, rerun, the timeline re-derives. The 216.9/220.2
one is a mid-stanza pause with no seam to hang on, so the script now takes a gap
anywhere, not only at section starts. Twice a corrected gap start landed within
0.35s of the previous line's end and I moved the end to meet it rather than
leave an unplayable sliver; both numbers are inside your tolerance.

Two things the spans turned up that change the video, not just the file:

- Each weld holds the stage only 10.6-13.9s, but catching a bottle needs
  1.2*K/4fps — Impact of AI needs 3m32s. Every bottle clears its budget ONLY
  because it stays up after its stanza passes. "The old ones keep playing" is
  load-bearing, not decorative.
- A loop dies below 2.0 px/module = 146px delivered. In a 1920 master that means
  tiles >= 328px to survive a 480p viewer and >= 438px for 360p. The outro's
  nine-up grid at 330px dies at 360p. If the outro is meant to be scannable, the
  nine cannot all be small at once.

On bottling the audio: sealing and QR-rendering come apart here. As a loop it is
impossible — 18.6MB is K=145,799, a 15.2-hour loop needing 12.1 hours of
filming, 79x too big even for the whole runtime, and compression does not close
that. As a seal it is easy and is what you actually want on screen: a 353-byte
receipt naming the audio by sha256 with version/supersedes fields, rendered at
renders/lucky-sevens/audio-seal/ as a 7-second loop catchable in 2.1s. Prior and
new masters seal to different receipts that can name each other, so re-sealing a
new version costs one re-render of that one small loop.

Still yours: the six long estimated starts (listed in the README), whether
GAP_MIN should rise to drop the eleven short inferred gaps, whether the screed
shows the chorus words or stays wordless, and the two ¿? spans, which I guessed
one way and 🎶 the other.

Not in this PR: the mock and the renders live in the FCCN-ANTIBODY workspace,
which is not a repo. The mock now carries the real cue times and has a "force
outro" checkbox, since the outro runs past the end of the audio and playback
cannot otherwise reach it.
